  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'A linearly polarised electromagnetic wave is incident upon a perfectly conducting ground plane at an angle. What happens to the polarisation of the reflected wave? Justify your answer. (5 marks)', 'The reflected wave is still linearly polarised. At a perfect conductor the tangential electric field must be zero, so all the energy is reflected (|Γ| = 1) and the tangential field components reverse phase (180°). Both the perpendicular (TE) and parallel (TM) components are reflected with equal magnitude and a fixed phase relation, so their combination stays linear; only the direction of the polarisation is mirrored about the plane of incidence. (By contrast, a circularly polarised wave would have its handedness reversed.)', null, '[{"point": "Reflected wave remains linearly polarised (total reflection, |Γ| = 1)", "marks": 2}, {"point": "Justification: tangential E must be zero at a perfect conductor, so tangential components reverse phase; both perpendicular and parallel components reflect with equal magnitude", "marks": 2}, {"point": "Orientation mirrored; (circular would reverse handedness)", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'subject-specialisation'),
